# PanoSETI Environment Configuration -- new stack alongside old software's gRPC
#
# Use this instead of .env.example when the old (mount-metadata-branch)
# software's telemetry gRPC service (control/daemons/capture_telemetry_
# service.py) might also be running on this machine -- e.g. during the
# transition period before test-pseti fully replaces it. Copy to `.env`
# and fill in the real values, same as .env.example.
#
# Verified live (UCB test rig, 2026-07-16): redeployed the new stack's
# headnode-server with HEADNODE_GRPC_PORT=50061, started a real gRPC
# server on the old software's actual default (GRPC_PORT unset -> 50051,
# the exact line the old telemetry service uses), and confirmed both
# simultaneously reachable and fully independent -- `pseti grpc --port
# 50061 stat` reported daq_data/telemetry SERVING, a plain gRPC health
# check against 50051 reported SERVING. No collision, no code changes
# needed on either side -- purely an env var choice.
#
# THE ONE THING THAT MATTERS: HEADNODE_GRPC_PORT must be set here,
# explicitly, to something the old software isn't using. Leaving it
# unset does NOT default to 50051-vs-something-else safely -- both the
# new stack's server bind and every client fall through to the legacy
# GRPC_PORT var if HEADNODE_GRPC_PORT isn't set (see .env.example's
# "COEXISTENCE GOTCHA" note), so an unset HEADNODE_GRPC_PORT plus a
# GRPC_PORT meant for the old software collides both stacks onto the
# same port instead of separating them.
#
# The old software's own port is controlled by ITS GRPC_PORT env var in
# whatever shell/service definition starts capture_telemetry_service.py
# -- leave it unset (defaults to 50051) or set it explicitly; either way,
# just make sure HEADNODE_GRPC_PORT below is a different value.

# ==============================================================================
# Common Configuration
# ==============================================================================

# PSETI_CONFIG=/path/to/panoseti-software/control/configs/lick

# ==============================================================================
# gRPC / Headnode Networking -- the coexistence-relevant part
# ==============================================================================

# REQUIRED here (not just recommended): must differ from whatever the old
# software's telemetry service resolves to (its GRPC_PORT, or 50051 if
# GRPC_PORT is unset for it). 50061 is the value this was actually tested
# with; any value that isn't 50051 (or the old software's explicit
# GRPC_PORT, if it has one) works.
HEADNODE_GRPC_PORT=50061

# DAQ nodes are unaffected by this scenario unless a DAQ node's gRPC
# server is co-located with old software too -- same rule applies there
# if so. Otherwise leave at the normal default.
DAQNODE_GRPC_PORT=50051

# Do NOT set GRPC_PORT here even to "reserve" 50051 for the old software
# -- per the gotcha above, the new stack reads it too as a fallback.
# HEADNODE_GRPC_PORT above is sufficient on its own.

# This machine's real IP -- required by `pseti admin deploy` for headnode (Alloy log shipping)
# HEADNODE_IP=192.168.88.103

# ==============================================================================
# Everything else
# ==============================================================================
# All other variables (PSETI_STATE, PSETI_RUNS_DIR, DAQ_DATA_DIR,
# PSETI_DATA_DIR, REDIS_HOST, LOKI_URL, PSETI_ROOT/PSETI_CONTROL/...) work
# identically to .env.example -- see that file for the full reference.
